      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To Evaluate the clinical outcome of patients with acute coronary syndrome (ACS) in the Coronary Care Unit (CCU) over three decades in Dunedin, New Zealand."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Registry study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING AND PATIENTS",
          "content": "all consecutive patients (n = 3,013) with ACS admitted to the CCU from 1979 to 1981 (n = 966) and from 1989 to 1991 (n = 1470) were included prospectively. Data on ACS patients managed in the CCU in 2001-2002 (n = 577) were obtained via medical chart review."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "There was a rising proportion of older (> or = 75 years of age) patients with ACS (3.8% in 1979-1981, 15.2% in 1989-1991 and 25.6% in 2001-2002, P < 0.0005). However, we observed a progressive reduction of in-hospital mortality for ACS (10.7, 7.3 and 5.0%, P < 0.005) and for ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) (18.4, 16.1 and 6.6%, P < 0.005). The progressive fall in mortality rate was also observed amongst older patients, both for ACS (27, 19.2 and 11.5%, P = 0.011) and for STEMI (34.8, 30.9 and 15.4%, P < 0.005). Of concern, only 10% of patients presented within 1 h of symptom onset and 50% within 5 h, and this has not changed over three decades. The variables associated with < 5 h from symptom onset to presentation were men [odds ratio (OR) 1.25, 95% confidence interval (CI) 1.10-1.42, P = 0.001], a history of ischaemic heart disease (OR 1.25, 95% CI 1.09-1.43, P = 0.002) and STEMI (OR 1.41, 95% CI 1.18-1.67, P < 0.0001). Advanced age was not a predictor for late presentation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Over the past three decades, more old patients were treated in the CCU. However, there was a decline in hospital mortality, particularly for STEMI. Further efforts are required to decrease the time to presentation."
        }
      ]},
